const REPO = 'opentela-ai/vkernels';

const COMMON = [
  'You are working in a managed git worktree of ' + REPO + ' (your cwd). Branch from the current HEAD of this worktree; do NOT checkout/push main; do NOT close/reopen any GitHub issue; do NOT modify any external repo (opentela-ai/cookbook, the private vLLM fork, sglang).',
  'First investigate: gh issue view <N> --repo ' + REPO + ' --comments  (read the FULL body and all comments; they carry the acceptance criteria, prior verdicts, and file pointers).',
  'Environment facts: this machine has an NVIDIA GB10 (sm_121) GPU only — gfx942/MI300A kernels cannot be executed here. The default python3 cannot import torch/pytest; look for the repo venv (e.g. .venv, or check meta/scripts and CI files for the interpreter used). Many compiler tests are bare-env numpy/fp64 suites that run without torch. Never fabricate on-GPU perf numbers; clearly separate locally-verified facts from pending on-cluster verification.',
  'Deliverable: implement the in-repo portion of the issue, run the relevant test subset until green, push a branch named issue-<N>-<short-slug> to origin (git push origin HEAD), and open a PR against main with gh pr create. PR body: what changed, evidence (commands + results), explicit mapping to the issue acceptance boxes (checked only if verified on real hardware), and residual risks. Use "Refs #<N>" in the title/body (NOT "Closes") unless every acceptance box is hardware-verified; open as --draft when on-cluster verification is still required.',
  'Final reply must include: PR URL (or precise reason no PR was possible), branch name, files touched, exact tests run with pass/fail counts, and what was deliberately left for on-cluster work.'
].join('\n');

const tasks = [
  { key: 'issue-137', issue: 137, task: COMMON + '\n\nISSUE 137 — perf(dsa_sparse_fwd_split): beat the full-topk 156 us / 430 GB/s floor. Prior state: the double-buffered kernel landed via PR #106 (gated behind an LDS-residency crossover; wins 13.4% at topk=2048/split=16) but at split=64 the gate correctly falls back to serial: 157.0 us / 428 GB/s. Deeper LDS staging cannot beat TLP at 4096 blocks (db kernel 9.3 KB/block LDS caps residency at 7 blocks/CU vs ~32 serial).\nImplement one or both levers from the issue: (a) wider per-key vector loads in the serial split-partial kernel (more bytes/key per load instruction on gfx942 HIP), (b) merge the combine step into the per-split partial kernel (one fewer launch + one fewer full read). Find the kernel under src/c/vkernels/kernels/ (grep for dsa_sparse_fwd_split) and its tests (grep tests/ for dsa_sparse). Correctness bar: the 17-row device-vs-oracle suite must stay semantically exact (log2-domain merge exact; uneven splits included) — you cannot run the gfx942 suite here, so keep the change mathematically conservative, add/extend whatever CPU-build test coverage exists for this kernel, and re-derive the dsa_sparse_fwd_split_for recommendation if the optimum could shift. Update docs/performance/dsa/gfx942.md with a clearly-marked "pending on-cluster measurement" section, not invented numbers. Open as draft.' },
  { key: 'issue-138', issue: 138, task: COMMON + '\n\nISSUE 138 — perf(mhc_pre): reach <=50 us/layer via gate change at NSLICE>=16. Prior state: PR #105 landed the blocked-order column split (NSLICE=8 passes the current PRE_THRESH=1e-4 gate at 68.8 us; NSLICE=64 would hit 26.6 us but its split residual ~1.1e-4 exceeds 1e-4 — that residual is the sqrsum tree-reduce / the oracle own fp32 associativity deviation, NOT a kernel bug). Known trap: blocked drift is seed-dependent (ns=2/16/32 failed 1e-4 on the measured seed, ns=4/8 passed) — a naive threshold bump would be seed-fragile.\nImplement the gate revision: an fp64-reference comparison path and/or a blocked-aware reduction (e.g. compare the blocked sqrsum tree against an fp64 oracle with its own tolerance, or restructure the reduction so the comparison is order-stable), such that NSLICE>=16 is admissible without weakening real-error detection. Keep the 1e-4 oracle-chain gate available (flag/env) for the strict regime. Find the parity/gate code (grep for mhc_pre, PRE_THRESH) plus tests and meta/scripts/run_dsa_mhc_bench_mi300.sh. Add tests that demonstrate: NSLICE>=16 passes the new gate across multiple seeds, and an intentionally corrupted kernel still FAILS the gate. Update docs/performance/mhc/gfx942.md and docs/kernels-reference.md (document the new gate policy). You cannot measure on MI300A here — the perf claim stays pending; the gate semantics and tests are the deliverable. Open as draft if the 50 us target is unverifiable locally, otherwise ready.' },
